\subsection{E3: three-parameter inverse design}
\label{sec:e3}

The synthetic target is
$(d^{*},k^{*},\tau^{*})=(480~\mathrm{nm},\,0.72,\,300~\mathrm{ns})$; the
starting point is $(300~\mathrm{nm},\,0.35,\,1.5~\mu\mathrm{s})$. With the
full observable set of Eq.~\eqref{eq:observables}, L-BFGS driven by exact IFT
gradients reduces the loss from $6.45\times10^{-3}$ to
$3.32\times10^{-13}$ in 44 function/gradient evaluations (106~s). The
recovered values are $d=479.990$~nm, $k=0.720015$ and
$\tau=3.0000\times10^{-7}$~s, i.e.\ relative errors of
$2.02\times10^{-5}$, $2.12\times10^{-5}$ and
$3.93\times10^{-7}$ in $\log\tau$ (Table~\ref{tab:inverse}). All three
parameters are recovered to within 0.002\%.

This result depends on the inclusion of $R(\lambda)$ and $T(\lambda)$. With
$J$--$V$ and EQE alone, a thinner and more strongly absorbing layer produces
nearly indistinguishable electrical observables, and the $(d,k)$ pair is
only weakly identifiable. Reflectometry and transmission measurements are
routine and, because they are computed directly by the TMM recursion, they
add no DD solves. The identifiability analysis is itself a useful output of
having exact gradients.

